registerChapter({
  id: 'locality',
  num: '24',
  phase: 'Phase 5 · Locality and the frame rule',
  title: 'What "local" has to mean',
  blurb: 'The rule that makes small specifications usable needs one fact about commands that no assertion can supply. This unit finds it by starting the proof and reading the two goals that will not close.',

  orient: {
    youWill: [
      "State the frame rule, and say why here it is a theorem about <code>Exec</code> rather than a rule you may adopt.",
      "Write the textbook locality condition as a Lean <code>def</code> and read it as a square that commutes: run then extend, or extend then run.",
      "Say why its conclusion is an existential, and meet the equivalent version that has none.",
      "Start the frame proof, reach two goals nothing in the context can reach, and say what each of them is asking for.",
      "Derive the missing conjunct <code>Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame</code> rather than be handed it, and say why a claim about the command belongs in the definition of <i>local</i>.",
      "Prove locality for the three commands that leave the heap as they found it.",
      "Refute the frame rule for a fact about a program variable, in Lean, with a one-command program.",
      "State the non-interference condition semantically, and say why the textbook syntactic one cannot be written down here at all.",
      "Discharge it for nothing for every assertion built from <code>↦</code>, <code>emp</code> and <code>∗</code> — and find the assertion at which that route stops."
    ],
    needs: [
      "Unit 23: three rules stated on one cell each, and the three questions that page closed on.",
      "Unit 22: <code>Hoare</code>, and that proving a triple means exhibiting a final state together with a derivation that reaches it.",
      "Unit 19: <code>Exec</code>, inversion with <code>cases … with | ctor</code>, and that <code>load</code>, <code>write</code> and <code>free</code> each carry a premise about the heap they run on.",
      "Unit 14: the six slots of <code>∗</code>. Unit 09: <code>union_of_some</code>. Unit 08: <code>Heap.disjoint</code> as a disjunction at each address."
    ],
    payoff: "Everything left in this module is the proof of one theorem, and the two definitions on this page are its two hypotheses. Neither is guessable: the first is one conjunct longer than the version in the literature, and the second exists because a one-command program refutes the rule without it."
  },

  blocks: [

    /* ===================================================== the rule wanted === */

    {t:'p', h:"Question (ii) is the one that asks for a definition, so take it first. A command runs on a small heap and finishes. You want to conclude that it also runs on a bigger heap, finishes, and leaves the extra part exactly as it found it. Nothing proved so far says that of any command, and nothing in the assertion language could: <code>∗</code> is a statement about how a heap comes apart, and this is a statement about what a command <i>does</i> to one."},

    {t:'p', h:"Here is the rule that fact would buy, in the notation the last unit used. It is also the answer to question (i): the conjunct the load&rsquo;s postcondition had and the free&rsquo;s precondition did not want is an <code>R</code>, and this is the rule that carries it across a command that has no business with it."},

    {t:'txt', cap:"The frame rule. <code>R</code> is arbitrary — any assertion at all. Read downwards it says a specification proved about a small heap may be reused inside a large one; read upwards it says you may set the untouched part of memory aside and forget about it while you reason about the rest.",
     src:"            { P }   c   { Q }\n     ─────────────────────────────────\n        { P ∗ R }   c   { Q ∗ R }"},

    {t:'p', h:"In a textbook that line is an axiom of the proof system, and the question of whether it is true is answered elsewhere, if at all. Here it cannot be an axiom, because a triple is not a token: <code>Hoare P c Q</code> unfolds to a claim with <code>Exec</code> in it. Proving the conclusion means producing, for every store and every heap satisfying <code>P ∗ R</code>, a final state and a derivation reaching it. The only derivation you have runs on the part of the heap that <code>P</code> owns. Turning it into one that runs on the whole is not a step of logic — whether it can be taken depends on which commands the language has."},

    {t:'p', h:"So write down the least you would have to know. You need a run on the big heap. You need its final heap to come apart into the piece your small run produced and the frame, unchanged. And you need its final store to be the store your small run produced, or <code>Q</code> will not transfer from one to the other. Three requirements, and they are the definition."},

    /* ================================================= the property, written === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The property, written down'},

    {t:'anat', tag:'verified',
     src:"def HeapLocalWeak (c : Cmd) : Prop :=\n  ∀ σ h hFrame s',\n    Heap.disjoint h hFrame →\n    Exec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s' →\n    ∃ r : State,\n      Exec c ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ r ∧\n      r.store = s'.store ∧\n      r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame",
     parts:[
       {m:'∀ σ h hFrame s\'', h:"Four things quantified and none of them optional. <code>σ</code> and <code>h</code> are the state the command actually runs on; <code>hFrame</code> is the memory somebody else owns; <code>s'</code> is a state that run may reach. The property has to hold for <i>every</i> frame, because the frame rule is applied by a caller who chooses <code>R</code>, and for every reachable <code>s'</code>, because <code>Exec</code> is a relation and does not promise there is only one."},
       {m:'Heap.disjoint h hFrame →', h:"The caller&rsquo;s side of the bargain. Without it <code>Heap.union h hFrame</code> is not <i>the two pieces put together</i> but a left-biased override: at an address both heaps define, the answer comes from <code>h</code> and the frame&rsquo;s cell is invisible. The sentence <i>the frame is still there afterwards</i> would then be saying nothing. The proof that genuinely spends this hypothesis is the next unit&rsquo;s <code>free</code> case."},
       {m:'Exec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s\' →', h:"The run you are given. Which way the implication points is the whole content of it: from a small run to a big one. That direction is what the frame rule needs and it is not symmetric — a run on the big heap may touch the frame, and then there is no corresponding small run at all."},
       {m:'∃ r : State,', h:"The conclusion has to exhibit a state. <code>Exec</code> relates states; there is no function to apply to <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> and no term to write on the right of an equation. So the property claims a state exists and then pins it down with two equations."},
       {m:'r.heap = Heap.union s\'.heap hFrame', h:"The equation that does the work. Not <i>the frame is somewhere in the final heap</i>, and not <i>the final heap contains the frame&rsquo;s cells</i>: the final heap <i>is</i> your final heap with the frame beside it, address for address. Exactness again — the same decision Unit 07 posed and Unit 13 took, now applied to a command instead of an assertion."}
     ]},

    {t:'p', h:"That is the definition as the literature states it, transcribed. It says a square commutes."},

    {t:'svg', cap:"Solid: what you are handed. Dashed: what locality claims exists. Going right is running the command; going down is putting the frame beside the heap. Locality says the two routes from the top left to the bottom right agree — and it says so in the only direction that can be asserted, since <code>r</code> has to be produced rather than computed.",
     src:"\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 660 250\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The commuting square: run then extend, or extend then run\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"ah27\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">\n      <path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n    </marker>\n  </defs>\n  <g class=\"dg\">\n    <text x=\"12\" y=\"46\" class=\"dg-t\">⟨σ, h⟩</text>\n    <line x1=\"110\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"398\" y2=\"41\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#ah27)\"/>\n    <text x=\"216\" y=\"31\" class=\"dg-note\">Exec c</text>\n    <text x=\"412\" y=\"46\" class=\"dg-t\">s&#8242;</text>\n\n    <line x1=\"36\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"36\" y2=\"146\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#ah27)\"/>\n    <text x=\"48\" y=\"108\" class=\"dg-note\">put hFrame beside it</text>\n    <line x1=\"418\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"418\" y2=\"146\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#ah27)\"/>\n    <text x=\"430\" y=\"108\" class=\"dg-note\">put hFrame beside it</text>\n\n    <text x=\"12\" y=\"172\" class=\"dg-t\">⟨σ, h.union hFrame⟩</text>\n    <line x1=\"196\" y1=\"167\" x2=\"398\" y2=\"167\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#ah27)\"/>\n    <text x=\"236\" y=\"157\" class=\"dg-note\">Exec c &#8212; some r</text>\n    <text x=\"412\" y=\"172\" class=\"dg-t\">⟨s&#8242;.store, s&#8242;.heap.union hFrame⟩</text>\n\n    <text x=\"12\" y=\"222\" class=\"dg-lab\">hypothesis: Heap.disjoint h hFrame</text>\n  </g>\n</svg>"},

    {t:'p', h:"Question (iii) asked for an operation that would not satisfy this, and the definition says which clause such an operation breaks. Take a command that asks whether an address is allocated — <code>x := allocated?(l)</code>, storing <code>1</code> if the heap answers at <code>l</code> and <code>0</code> if it does not. Run it on a heap without <code>l</code> and the store ends with <code>0</code> at <code>x</code>. Put beside that heap a frame that does hold <code>l</code>, and the same command ends with <code>1</code>. The clause that fails is <code>r.store = s'.store</code>, the one that looks like bookkeeping. Nothing about heaps and nothing about assertions rules such a command out. What rules it out here is a decision made in the semantics: a command that finds no cell at the address it was given has no run at all, so no command in this language can report on memory it was not handed. Locality is a property of the command set, not of the assertion language, which is why the two units after this one work through <code>Exec</code> one constructor at a time."},

    /* ============================================== the proof that strands === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The proof that will not close'},

    {t:'p', h:"A definition is worth what its consumers can do with it, so take the frame rule as a Lean statement and try to prove it, with <code>HeapLocalWeak c</code> as the only thing assumed about the command."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"Lean accepts this and reports exactly one thing: <code>declaration uses `sorry`</code>. Everything except the two holes went through. So the holes are not a proof that has gone wrong; they are the two facts the definition does not supply, isolated. Exercise stubs aside, this is the first of the two places in this course where a proof is displayed with a <code>sorry</code> in it; the other is its repair, four blocks below.",
     src:"example {P Q R : Assertion} {c : Cmd}\n    (hc : Hoare P c Q) (hlocal : HeapLocalWeak c) :\n    Hoare (P ∗ R) c (Q ∗ R) := by\n  intro σ h hstar\n  obtain ⟨hP, hR, hd, hu, hp, hr⟩ := hstar\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, hq⟩ := hc σ hP hp\n  obtain ⟨r, hrex, hrst, hrhp⟩ := hlocal σ hP hR s' hd hex\n  subst hu\n  refine ⟨r, hrex, s'.heap, hR, ?_, hrhp, ?_, ?_⟩\n  · sorry\n  · rw [hrst]; exact hq\n  · sorry"},

    {t:'p', h:"Four lines of it are unpacking. The precondition <code>(P ∗ R) σ h</code> gives up its six slots: two heaps, their disjointness, the equation cutting <code>h</code> in two, and the two assertions holding at their halves. The triple you already have, applied at <code>hP</code>, gives a final state <code>s'</code>, a derivation, and <code>Q</code> at <code>s'.heap</code>. Locality, applied at that derivation with <code>hR</code> as the frame, gives <code>r</code> and its two equations. <code>subst hu</code> then replaces <code>h</code> everywhere by <code>Heap.union hP hR</code>, which is what makes <code>hrex</code> a derivation about the heap the goal is asking about."},

    {t:'p', h:"The <code>refine</code> has eight slots because the goal is an existential whose payload is a conjunction whose second half is a <code>∗</code>: two slots for the state and its derivation, six for the star. Four of them are filled by hand — <code>r</code>, <code>hrex</code>, and the cut <code>s'.heap</code>, <code>hR</code>, which is the only cut available, since <code>hrhp</code> says <code>r.heap</code> is exactly that union. Of the four remaining, one is <code>hrhp</code> itself, one is <code>Q</code> transported along <code>hrst</code>, and two are holes."},

    {t:'state', cap:"The two goals left open, quoted as their <code>case</code> line and their <code>⊢</code> line. <code>refine_2</code> is missing because the middle bullet closed it. Both carry the same sixteen lines of context, which the fold below prints in full. That is the convention on this page: where a state shows fewer lines than Lean printed, the omitted ones are unchanged and the block says so. Nothing is reworded.",
     src:"case refine_1\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hR\n\ncase refine_3\n⊢ R r.store hR"},

    {t:'detail', title:'Both goals, with the whole context', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"Eighteen lines each, counting the <code>case</code> line and the goal, and the sixteen lines of context between them are identical. They are here so that the claim made about them in the main text — that no hypothesis reaches either goal — is one you can check rather than take."},
       {t:'state', cap:"The first hole.",
        src:"case refine_1\nP Q R : Assertion\nc : Cmd\nhc : Hoare P c Q\nhlocal : HeapLocalWeak c\nσ : Store\nhP hR : Heap\nhd : hP.disjoint hR\nhp : P σ hP\nhr : R σ hR\ns' : State\nhex : Exec c { store := σ, heap := hP } s'\nhq : Q s'.store s'.heap\nr : State\nhrex : Exec c { store := σ, heap := hP.union hR } r\nhrst : r.store = s'.store\nhrhp : r.heap = s'.heap.union hR\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hR"},
       {t:'state', cap:"The second. The context is the same list; only the last line differs.",
        src:"case refine_3\nP Q R : Assertion\nc : Cmd\nhc : Hoare P c Q\nhlocal : HeapLocalWeak c\nσ : Store\nhP hR : Heap\nhd : hP.disjoint hR\nhp : P σ hP\nhr : R σ hR\ns' : State\nhex : Exec c { store := σ, heap := hP } s'\nhq : Q s'.store s'.heap\nr : State\nhrex : Exec c { store := σ, heap := hP.union hR } r\nhrst : r.store = s'.store\nhrhp : r.heap = s'.heap.union hR\n⊢ R r.store hR"}
     ]},

    /* ------------------------------------------------ the first missing fact --- */

    {t:'h3', s:'Reading the first hole'},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hR</code>. The star in the postcondition demands that the two halves of the final heap do not overlap, and the two halves are the heap your run produced and the frame. Go through the context looking for a hypothesis that mentions both. <code>hd</code> relates <code>hP</code> to <code>hR</code> — the heap the run <i>started</i> on. <code>hex</code> says the run happened. <code>hrhp</code> says <code>r.heap</code> is the union, which is what is being cut, not evidence about the cut. Nothing connects <code>s'.heap</code> to <code>hR</code>."},

    {t:'p', h:"So the question is whether a run that starts on a heap disjoint from the frame has to end on one. For an arbitrary transformation of heaps it does not. Writing is enough to break it:"},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"Disjointness is not preserved by writing. The witness is the smallest one there is: the empty heap is disjoint from the cell at address <code>0</code>, and writing to address <code>0</code> makes it overlap. Nothing about heaps rules this out, because nothing about heaps says who is allowed to write where.",
     src:"example : ¬ (∀ (h hFrame : Heap) (l : Loc) (v : Val),\n    Heap.disjoint h hFrame → Heap.disjoint (Heap.write h l v) hFrame) := by\n  intro hall\n  have hd : Heap.disjoint Heap.empty (Heap.singleton 0 1) := disjoint_empty_left _\n  have h2 := hall Heap.empty (Heap.singleton 0 1) 0 7 hd 0\n  rcases h2 with hx | hx\n  · rw [write_same] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)\n  · rw [singleton_same] at hx; exact absurd hx (by simp)"},

    {t:'p', h:"What rules it out for <code>[l] := e</code> is <code>Exec.write</code>&rsquo;s premise, <code>hl : s.heap l = some old</code>. A command may only write where it already holds a cell, so a run that starts inside your own memory stays inside it. That is a fact about the semantics — it is legible in the constructor and nowhere else. It is not a fact about heaps, it is not a fact about <code>∗</code>, and there is no assertion you could add to <code>P</code> to supply it, because <code>P</code> constrains the initial heap and the goal is about the final one."},

    {t:'p', h:"A fact about the command that the frame rule cannot do without, and that the definition of <i>local</i> does not state, is a fact the definition is missing."},

    {t:'note', kind:'warn', title:'The conjunct that is not in the textbook',
     h:"The definition above is the standard one, and it is one conjunct short of what the frame rule needs. The gap is easy to miss on paper because the missing fact is true of every command anyone writes down, and on paper &ldquo;the frame is unchanged&rdquo; is read as including &ldquo;and still separate from the rest&rdquo;. A proof assistant does not read anything as including anything. The stuck goal is the only reliable audit of a definition. Run it before you trust one."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"One line longer. The conclusion is now a conjunction: first the disjointness the frame rule will consume, then the existential as before.",
     src:"def HeapLocal (c : Cmd) : Prop :=\n  ∀ σ h hFrame s',\n    Heap.disjoint h hFrame →\n    Exec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s' →\n    Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r : State,\n      Exec c ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ r ∧\n      r.store = s'.store ∧\n      r.heap = Heap.union s'.heap hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"Strengthening a definition costs something, and the price is paid by whoever proves it: every locality proof now owes a disjointness argument as well as a heap equation. That bill comes due twice, in the next unit, and both times the two halves are proved separately and for different reasons. At the use site it costs one name: the <code>obtain</code> takes a fifth, and the first hole is closed by it."},

    {t:'code', tag:'sketch', cap:"The same proof with the strengthened definition. One hole left.",
     src:"example {P Q R : Assertion} {c : Cmd}\n    (hc : Hoare P c Q) (hlocal : HeapLocal c) :\n    Hoare (P ∗ R) c (Q ∗ R) := by\n  intro σ h hstar\n  obtain ⟨hP, hR, hd, hu, hp, hr⟩ := hstar\n  obtain ⟨s', hex, hq⟩ := hc σ hP hp\n  obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hrex, hrst, hrhp⟩ := hlocal σ hP hR s' hd hex\n  subst hu\n  refine ⟨r, hrex, s'.heap, hR, hdEnd, hrhp, ?_, ?_⟩\n  · rw [hrst]; exact hq\n  · sorry"},

    {t:'p', h:"Reading the remaining hole takes a counterexample rather than an audit of the context, so leave it standing. The definition has now been written twice and strengthened once, and no command has been shown to satisfy either version. A definition nothing satisfies is not yet a definition, and the three easiest commands settle that in one shape."},

    /* ================================================== the three easy cases === */

    {t:'sec', s:'The three commands that are already done'},

    {t:'p', h:"Locality is a claim about <code>Exec</code>, and <code>Exec</code> has ten rules. Five of them are control flow and belong to a later unit, because none of them touches memory itself: each is proved from the locality of the commands inside it, and that argument is about composition rather than about heaps. Of the remaining five, <code>write</code> and <code>free</code> change the heap and are the whole of the next unit. The other three are <code>skip</code>, <code>assign</code> and <code>load</code>: they hand the heap back exactly as they received it, so the frame&rsquo;s cells are untouched for a reason that needs no argument."},

    {t:'p', h:"All three proofs are one shape. Introduce the six binders. Invert the run with <code>cases</code>, which replaces <code>s'</code> by the state the rule produces and so turns every occurrence of <code>s'.heap</code> in the goal into something concrete. Then supply the whole conclusion as one tuple: the disjointness you were handed, the state the big run lands in, the derivation, and two <code>rfl</code>s."},

    {t:'state', cap:"<code>heapLocal_skip</code> after <code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code>. The goal is the definition with its binders discharged, and <code>s'</code> is still an opaque state — which is why nothing in it can be closed yet.",
     src:"σ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\ns' : State\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nhex : Exec Cmd.skip { store := σ, heap := h } s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r, Exec Cmd.skip { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧ r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>load</code> is the one with content, and it is one lemma wide. The rule&rsquo;s premise is <code>s.heap l = some v</code> — the small heap holds <code>v</code> at <code>l</code> — and to build the big run you must produce the same premise about <code>Heap.union h hFrame</code>. That is <code>union_of_some</code>, from Unit 09: if the left heap answers at an address, the union answers the same. Read as a statement about programs it says the frame cannot change what a load reads, and the reason it is true is Unit 09&rsquo;s left bias: wherever the left heap answers, the union gives the same answer and never consults the right one."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'m8-1',
     name:'heapLocal_skip / heapLocal_assign / heapLocal_load',
     hard:false,
     why:"Three proofs of the property defined above, so that it stops being a wish and starts being something commands have. They are also the calibration for the next unit: these three are the cases with no content, and if they feel long, the two that change the heap will feel very long. Downstream, only one of the three is ever cited by name: <code>heapLocal_load</code>, once, in Unit 29, where a load is framed inside a two-command program. <code>heapLocal_skip</code> and <code>heapLocal_assign</code> are never called again — they are here because the composition results of Unit 26 take locality of the parts as hypotheses, and a theorem about parts is worth nothing until some parts have it.",
     setup:"Three theorems in one box. Each is <code>intro</code>, one <code>cases</code>, and a term; <code>load</code> takes one line more than that. The tuple you supply has five components in the order the definition lists them: the disjointness, the state <code>r</code>, the derivation, and the two equations. Both equations are <code>rfl</code> in all three proofs — if either is not, the state you named is the wrong one.",
     goal:"theorem heapLocal_skip : HeapLocal .skip := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem heapLocal_assign (x : Var) (e : Atom) : HeapLocal (.assign x e) := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem heapLocal_load (x : Var) (l : Loc) : HeapLocal (.load x l) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, each goal says: given a frame disjoint from <code>h</code> and a run of this command from <code>⟨σ, h⟩</code> to <code>s'</code>, the final heap of that run is still disjoint from the frame, and there is a state <code>r</code> reached by running the same command from <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> whose store is <code>s'</code>&rsquo;s and whose heap is <code>s'</code>&rsquo;s with the frame beside it.",
       "None of these three commands changes the heap. So the final heap of the small run is <code>h</code>, and the disjointness you are asked for is the disjointness you were given. The big run is the same rule applied to the big heap, and it lands on <code>h.union hFrame</code> — which is the union of <code>h</code> with the frame, so the second equation is an identity. For <code>load</code> there is one thing to check: the rule&rsquo;s premise has to hold of the big heap, not the small one.",
       "Two tactics and a term for <code>skip</code> and <code>assign</code>; <code>load</code> is one line longer. The tactic that makes <code>s'</code> concrete is <code>cases</code> — bare for the first two, whose rules carry nothing worth naming, and in the named form <code>cases hex with | load hl =&gt;</code> for <code>load</code>, whose rule carries a premise. The conclusion then goes in as one anonymous-constructor tuple. The only lemma any of the three cites is <code>union_of_some</code>, in <code>load</code>, on its last line.",
       "<code>intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex</code> opens all three and leaves the conjunction as the goal, with <code>s'</code> still opaque. After <code>cases hex</code>, <code>skip</code>&rsquo;s goal has <code>s'</code> replaced by <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }</code> throughout, and <code>exact ⟨hd, ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.skip, rfl, rfl⟩</code> closes it; <code>assign</code> is the same term with <code>Store.set σ x (e.eval σ)</code> for the store. <code>load</code> will not go in with a single <code>exact</code>, because the derivation needs a premise that is not in the context: build the tuple with <code>refine</code> and leave that one argument as <code>?_</code>. What comes back is the same lookup, at the union instead of at <code>h</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapLocal_skip : HeapLocal .skip := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex\n  exact ⟨hd, ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.skip, rfl, rfl⟩\n\ntheorem heapLocal_assign (x : Var) (e : Atom) : HeapLocal (.assign x e) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex\n  exact ⟨hd, ⟨Store.set σ x (e.eval σ), Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.assign, rfl, rfl⟩\n\ntheorem heapLocal_load (x : Var) (l : Loc) : HeapLocal (.load x l) := by\n  intro σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n  cases hex with\n  | load hl =>\n      refine ⟨hd, ⟨Store.set σ x _, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.load ?_, rfl, rfl⟩\n      exact union_of_some hFrame hl",
     solNote:"The underscore in <code>Store.set σ x _</code> is the value read from the heap. It has no name in the goal — <code>cases</code> introduced it as <code>v✝</code> — and it does not need one: the derivation you build in the next line determines it.",
     expl:"Each proof is the definition with its binders discharged, one inversion, and one tuple. The inversion is what makes the goal concrete: before it, <code>s'</code> is an arbitrary state and <code>s'.heap</code> could be anything; after it, <code>s'</code> is the state the rule produces and the two equations become identities. In <code>skip</code> and <code>assign</code> nothing else happens. In <code>load</code> the derivation cannot be handed over unchanged, because <code>Exec.load</code>&rsquo;s premise is about whichever heap it runs on, and this one runs on the union.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex', h:"Discharged the four binders and the two implications of <code>HeapLocal</code>, leaving the conjunction as the goal. The same line opens every locality proof in the course."},
       {tac:'cases hex', h:"Inversion. <code>Exec .skip</code> has exactly one rule, whose conclusion is <code>Exec .skip s s</code>, so the branch it produces has <code>s'</code> replaced everywhere by <code>{ store := σ, heap := h }</code>. The two equations in the goal are now between things that are equal by reduction."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨hd, ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.skip, rfl, rfl⟩', h:"Five components. <code>hd</code> is the disjointness — unchanged, since the heap is unchanged. <code>⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩</code> is the state you claim the big run reaches, given as a <code>State</code> literal. <code>Exec.skip</code> is the derivation. The two <code>rfl</code>s close <code>r.store = { store := σ, heap := h }.store</code> and <code>r.heap = { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.union hFrame</code>: those projections do not reduce in the display, but <code>exact</code> compares up to reduction and they are the same terms."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨hd, ⟨Store.set σ x (e.eval σ), Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.assign, rfl, rfl⟩', h:"The <code>assign</code> case, and only the second component differs from <code>skip</code>&rsquo;s: the store the big run ends in is <code>σ</code> with <code>x</code> set to the value of <code>e</code>, which is the same store the small run ended in, because an assignment reads the store and not the heap. Both equations are again <code>rfl</code>, this time against an eighteen-line goal — the fold below prints it."},
       {tac:'cases hex with | load hl =>', h:"The <code>load</code> rule carries a premise, and this form names it. <code>hl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some v✝</code> — the small heap holds something at <code>l</code>, and the value has an inaccessible name."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨hd, ⟨Store.set σ x _, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.load ?_, rfl, rfl⟩', h:"Everything except the premise of the derivation. <code>refine</code> rather than <code>exact</code> because the premise is not one of the hypotheses: the hole left behind is <code>⊢ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.heap l = some v✝</code>, the same lookup at the union."},
       {tac:'exact union_of_some hFrame hl', h:"Closed it. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl</code> has type <code>Heap.union h hFrame l = some v✝</code>; the goal displays the heap as a projection out of a state literal. They are the same term, and <code>exact</code> accepts it without any tactic to strip the projection."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'heapLocal_skip, in two moves',
        start:"⊢ HeapLocal Cmd.skip",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex',
           state:"σ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\ns' : State\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nhex : Exec Cmd.skip { store := σ, heap := h } s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r, Exec Cmd.skip { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧ r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"Nothing here can be closed. <code>s'</code> is opaque, so <code>s'.heap.disjoint hFrame</code> is a claim about a heap nobody has described."},
          {tac:'cases hex',
           state:"case skip\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\n⊢ { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec Cmd.skip { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store = { store := σ, heap := h }.store ∧ r.heap = { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.union hFrame",
           h:"<code>s'</code> is gone and the state it stood for is written out. The first conjunct is now <code>hd</code> with an unreduced projection in front of it; the two equations are identities once <code>r</code> is chosen."},
          {tac:'exact ⟨hd, ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.skip, rfl, rfl⟩', state:'No goals.', h:"One term for five obligations."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'p', h:"The <code>load</code> case differs at one point, and that point is the whole of it. After the inversion, <code>refine</code> takes everything except the derivation&rsquo;s premise and leaves this:"},
       {t:'state', cap:"The hole left by <code>Exec.load ?_</code>. The heap is written as a projection out of the state literal that <code>Exec.load</code> was applied at, and the value <code>v✝</code> is the one the inversion produced. <code>union_of_some hFrame hl</code> has exactly this type up to reduction.",
        src:"case load\nx : Var\nl : Loc\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nv✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some v✝\n⊢ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.heap l = some v✝"},
       {t:'detail', title:'The load proof, all four states', tag:'aside', open:false,
         blocks:[
           {t:'p', h:"The two middle states are the ones that do not belong in the main line — the first of them runs to fifteen lines, seven of which are one goal — and seeing them consecutively is what shows how much of this proof <code>refine</code> is doing. It takes a goal in which every occurrence of <code>s'</code> has been replaced by a two-deep state literal, and leaves a single lookup."},
           {t:'trace', title:'heapLocal_load, tactic by tactic',
            start:"x : Var\nl : Loc\n⊢ HeapLocal (Cmd.load x l)",
            steps:[
              {tac:'intro σ h hFrame s\' hd hex',
               state:"x : Var\nl : Loc\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\ns' : State\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nhex : Exec (Cmd.load x l) { store := σ, heap := h } s'\n⊢ s'.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.load x l) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧ r.store = s'.store ∧ r.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame",
               h:"The same opening as <code>skip</code>, one command later. Nothing is provable yet: <code>s'</code> is a variable, so both equations are between terms that have no relation to each other."},
              {tac:'cases hex with | load hl =>',
               state:"case load\nx : Var\nl : Loc\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nv✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some v✝\n⊢ { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x v✝, heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.heap.disjoint hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.load x l) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store = { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x v✝, heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.store ∧\n          r.heap =\n            { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x v✝, heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.heap.union\n              hFrame",
               h:"Two things arrived. <code>v✝</code> and <code>hl</code>: the value the small heap holds at <code>l</code>, and the evidence. And <code>s'</code> became the state <code>Exec.load</code> produces, which is why the goal went from three printed lines to seven — the mathematics did not change, only how much of it is written out."},
              {tac:'refine ⟨hd, ⟨Store.set σ x _, Heap.union h hFrame⟩, Exec.load ?_, rfl, rfl⟩',
               state:"case load\nx : Var\nl : Loc\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\nv✝ : Val\nhl : { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some v✝\n⊢ { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.heap l = some v✝",
               h:"Seven lines of goal down to one. The disjointness went in as <code>hd</code>, both equations closed by reduction, and what is left is the derivation&rsquo;s premise — which is the only part of the proof that is about this command rather than about the shape of the definition."},
              {tac:'exact union_of_some hFrame hl', state:'No goals.', h:"The frame cannot change what a load reads."}
            ],
            done:'No goals.'}
         ]},
       {t:'detail', title:'What the assign case looks like before you close it', tag:'aside', open:false,
         blocks:[
           {t:'p', h:"<code>assign</code>&rsquo;s proof is the shortest of the three and its goal state is by far the longest, because the state the rule produces is built out of two projections of a state literal and every occurrence is printed in full."},
           {t:'state', cap:"After <code>cases hex</code> in <code>heapLocal_assign</code>. Eighteen lines, of which the mathematics is: the heap did not change, and the store gained one entry. The term that closes it is one line. The gap between what a goal says and how much of the screen it takes is at its widest here. It is not a difficulty: the term is written from the definition, and never reads the display.",
            src:"case assign\nx : Var\ne : Atom\nσ : Store\nh hFrame : Heap\nhd : h.disjoint hFrame\n⊢ { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e),\n            heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.heap.disjoint\n      hFrame ∧\n    ∃ r,\n      Exec (Cmd.assign x e) { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame } r ∧\n        r.store =\n            { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e),\n                heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.store ∧\n          r.heap =\n            { store := { store := σ, heap := h }.store.set x (Atom.eval { store := σ, heap := h }.store e),\n                    heap := { store := σ, heap := h }.heap }.heap.union\n              hFrame"}
         ]}
     ],
     pitfall:"Skipping the <code>cases</code> and going straight to the term. It looks reasonable — <code>skip</code> changes nothing, so why invert? — and Lean reports two failures in the one tuple. The first component: <code>Application type mismatch: The argument hd has type h.disjoint hFrame but is expected to have type s'.heap.disjoint hFrame</code>. The last: <code>The argument rfl has type ?m.27 = ?m.27 but is expected to have type { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.heap = s'.heap.union hFrame</code>. Both are the same complaint. While <code>s'</code> is a variable, <code>s'.heap</code> is a term with no connection to <code>h</code>, so the disjointness you hold is about the wrong heap and no reflexivity can unify the two sides. The inversion is not bookkeeping here; it is the step that makes the goal true.",
     variants:"Hand <code>Exec.load</code> the premise you were given rather than the one about the union — <code>Exec.load hl</code> instead of <code>Exec.load ?_</code> — and Lean reports <code>The argument hl has type { store := σ, heap := h }.heap l = some v✝ but is expected to have type { store := σ, heap := h.union hFrame }.heap l = some ?m.107</code>. Those two are not the same statement, and the theorem that closes the gap is the one that says a read cannot be disturbed by a frame. &middot; Delete the <code>Heap.disjoint h hFrame</code> hypothesis and keep the conjunct it pays for, and all three proofs stop: the first component has nothing to be, and Lean says <code>don&rsquo;t know how to synthesize placeholder for argument `left`</code> over <code>⊢ { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.disjoint hFrame</code>. These three commands do not <i>use</i> disjointness, but they do have to hand it back, so they need to have been given it. The next unit is where a proof consults it. &middot; Put the frame on the <i>left</i> of both unions — <code>Heap.union hFrame h</code> in the derivation, <code>Heap.union hFrame s'.heap</code> in the equation. <code>skip</code> and <code>assign</code> do not notice. <code>load</code> breaks at one line: <code>union_of_some hFrame hl has type { store := σ, heap := h }.heap.union hFrame l = some v✝ but is expected to have type { store := σ, heap := hFrame.union h }.heap l = some v✝</code>. Nothing sends the first to the second in general, because union is left-biased and the two heaps genuinely differ wherever the arguments overlap. Inserting <code>rw [union_comm (disjoint_symm hd)]</code> before the last line repairs it — and that is the first moment any of these three proofs <i>consults</i> <code>hd</code> instead of handing it back. Which side the frame goes on is a choice, and the right-hand side is the one that costs nothing. &middot; Weaken the last equation to say only that the final heap is your final heap with <i>something</i> beside it, and the property stops mentioning the frame it was handed: a command that emptied the frame and left different cells in its place would satisfy the weakened form. No command in this language does that, which is exactly why the strength has to be written into the definition rather than read off the language."
    },

    {t:'detail', title:'The version with no existential', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"The conclusion exhibits a state and then pins it down with two equations. Since the two equations pin it down completely, you could write the state out instead and drop the existential entirely."},
       {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"The same property with the final state given as a literal. The two definitions are equivalent, and the proof of that is four lines each way.",
        src:"def HeapLocalPair (c : Cmd) : Prop :=\n  ∀ σ h hFrame s',\n    Heap.disjoint h hFrame →\n    Exec c ⟨σ, h⟩ s' →\n      Heap.disjoint s'.heap hFrame ∧\n      Exec c ⟨σ, Heap.union h hFrame⟩ ⟨s'.store, Heap.union s'.heap hFrame⟩\n\ntheorem heapLocal_pair_iff (c : Cmd) : HeapLocal c ↔ HeapLocalPair c := by\n  constructor\n  · intro hc σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n    obtain ⟨hdEnd, r, hrex, hst, hhp⟩ := hc σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n    refine ⟨hdEnd, ?_⟩\n    have heq : r = ⟨s'.store, Heap.union s'.heap hFrame⟩ := by rw [← hst, ← hhp]\n    rw [heq] at hrex\n    exact hrex\n  · intro hc σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n    obtain ⟨hdEnd, hrex⟩ := hc σ h hFrame s' hd hex\n    exact ⟨hdEnd, _, hrex, rfl, rfl⟩"},
       {t:'p', h:"So the existential is a choice, not a necessity, and the usual reason given for it — that <code>Exec</code> is a relation and there is no final state to compute — is only half the story. There is no final state to <i>compute</i>, but there is one to <i>write down</i>, because the two equations say what it is."},
       {t:'p', h:"What the existential buys is the exercise above. In the pair form the last line of each proof would be a single derivation term whose type Lean must match against a state literal you wrote, all at once; in the existential form the state is one slot and the two equations are two more, and each of them fails separately and reports separately. The gain is larger for the consumer: a proof that <i>uses</i> locality gets <code>r</code> as a name and its two equations as hypotheses it can rewrite with, which is exactly what the frame proof above does with <code>hrst</code>. Under the pair form it would have to take the whole state apart again at every use."}
     ]},

    /* ============================================== the second hole =========== */

    {t:'sec', s:'The hole that is not about memory'},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>⊢ R r.store hR</code>, with <code>hr : R σ hR</code> in the context. The heap argument is <code>hR</code> in both — the frame, which the command never touched, by the last equation in the definition of <i>local</i>. The store argument is the only thing that differs. So the goal is asking: an assertion held of the frame heap before the command ran; does it still hold after?"},

    {t:'p', h:"If <code>R</code> is a claim about the frame heap, the answer is yes and there is nothing to prove: the frame heap has not moved. But <code>R</code> is an arbitrary <code>Assertion</code>, and an <code>Assertion</code> takes a store as well as a heap. Nothing stops it from ignoring its heap argument entirely and talking about a program variable, and Unit 17 built the embedding that does exactly that. Frame such an assertion onto a command that assigns to the variable it talks about, and the rule states something false."},

    {t:'txt', cap:"The frame rule at <code>R</code> = <i>the store holds 0 at x</i>. The premise is a triple you can prove in four lines. The conclusion says that after the assignment, <code>x</code> holds 1 and, separately, <code>x</code> holds 0.",
     src:"          { x = 0 }   x := 1   { x = 1 }\n   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────\n     { (x = 0) ∗ (x = 0) }   x := 1   { (x = 1) ∗ (x = 0) }"},

    {t:'p', h:"This is not a refutation of nothing. A conclusion can only be false if its precondition is satisfiable, and this one is: Unit 17&rsquo;s <code>pure</code> owns the empty heap, so <code>pure φ ∗ pure φ</code> holds at the empty heap under the cut into two empty heaps, with <code>φ</code> true in the store. There is a state satisfying the precondition, the command runs from it without faulting, and the postcondition fails at the state it reaches. Every part of that is checkable, which is the next exercise."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x51',
     name:'The store counterexample, as a refutation',
     hard:true,
     why:"This is where <code>Preserves</code> is earned. The definition on the other side of this exercise looks like an arbitrary side condition until you have watched the rule fail without it, and the failure is small enough to hold in your head: one command, one variable, two lines of arithmetic. It is also the third proof rule this course refutes outright rather than doubts — Unit 00 took the classical assignment rule, Unit 07 took the free rule under the loose reading of ownership — and the shape of the argument is Unit 00&rsquo;s: exhibit a state satisfying the precondition, run the command, contradict the postcondition where it lands.",
     setup:"<b>A design exercise: write the statement yourself.</b> Two parts, in one theorem joined by <code>∧</code>. Part one: the unframed triple <code>{ x = 0 } x := 1 { x = 1 }</code> holds — say it with <code>Hoare</code> and Unit 17&rsquo;s <code>pure</code>. Part two: the framed triple, with the frame assertion being the same store fact, does <i>not</i> hold. The editor starts you with a placeholder; delete it and write your own <code>theorem frame_needs_preserves …</code>. Your own text is what gets checked, so a different correct statement passes too, and the <i>variants</i> panel names two of them.",
     goal:"-- Delete the line below and write your own `theorem frame_needs_preserves …`.\n-- Part 1: the triple { x = 0 } x := 1 { x = 1 } holds.\n-- Part 2: framing it with the assertion `x = 0` gives a triple that does not.\nexample : True := by",
     hints:[
       "One theorem whose body is <code>A ∧ ¬ B</code>. <code>A</code> is a triple: precondition <i>the store holds 0 at <code>x</code></i>, command <i>assign the constant 1 to <code>x</code></i>, postcondition <i>the store holds 1 at <code>x</code></i>. <code>B</code> is that same triple with one assertion starred onto both ends, and <code>B</code> is the half that is false. What is left to you: which assertion does the framing, and what the theorem quantifies over.",
       "The frame rule can only fail where the frame notices the command, so the framing assertion has to be a claim about the <i>store</i>. It also has to own no memory, or the framed precondition would demand a heap that the unframed triple never had. Take it to be the precondition itself. Part one is then proved the way every triple is: exhibit the final state, give the derivation, check the postcondition there. Part two is a negation, so assume the framed triple, feed it a state satisfying its precondition, and read both halves of the postcondition at the state it lands in — the star cuts the <i>heap</i> and hands the same store to both halves, so they assert <i>1 at <code>x</code></i> and <i>0 at <code>x</code></i> of one store.",
       "The store fact that owns the empty heap is <code>pure</code>, and the assertion is <code>pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)</code>. Cutting the empty heap into two empty heaps needs <code>disjoint_empty_left</code> and <code>union_empty_left</code>, the second of them reversed. The statement is<br><code>theorem frame_needs_preserves (x : Var) :<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Hoare (pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 0)) (.assign x (.const 1)) (pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 1))<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;∧ ¬ Hoare (pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 0) ∗ pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 0))<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(.assign x (.const 1))<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 1) ∗ pure (fun σ =&gt; σ x = 0))</code><br>Only <code>x</code> is quantified. Both halves are needed: without the first, the second refutes a rule whose premise might have been false anyway.",
       "<code>constructor</code> splits the two halves. The first opens <code>intro σ h ⟨_, he⟩</code> — the precondition is a <code>pure</code>, so a pair, and only its second component is used. The second opens <code>intro hall</code> and then instantiates: <code>hall (fun _ =&gt; 0) Heap.empty ⟨Heap.empty, Heap.empty, disjoint_empty_left _, (union_empty_left _).symm, ⟨rfl, rfl⟩, ⟨rfl, rfl⟩⟩</code> is the precondition satisfied at the empty heap, cut into two empty heaps."
     ],
     sol:"theorem frame_needs_preserves (x : Var) :\n    Hoare (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)) (.assign x (.const 1)) (pure (fun σ => σ x = 1))\n    ∧ ¬ Hoare (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ x = 0))\n              (.assign x (.const 1))\n              (pure (fun σ => σ x = 1) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)) := by\n  constructor\n  · intro σ h ⟨_, he⟩\n    refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x 1, h⟩, Exec.assign, ?_, he⟩\n    show Store.set σ x 1 x = 1\n    simp [Store.set]\n  · intro hall\n    obtain ⟨s', hex, h₁, h₂, _, _, hq, hr⟩ :=\n      hall (fun _ => 0) Heap.empty\n        ⟨Heap.empty, Heap.empty, disjoint_empty_left _, (union_empty_left _).symm,\n         ⟨rfl, rfl⟩, ⟨rfl, rfl⟩⟩\n    cases hex\n    have e0 : Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1 x = 0 := hr.1\n    rw [show Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1 x = 1 from by simp [Store.set]] at e0\n    exact absurd e0 (by simp)",
     solNote:"If your own statement differed and Lean accepted your proof of it, you were right; the checker runs your text. Compare the two anyway, because which parts are quantified — the variable, and nothing else — is the part that decides what the theorem is worth.",
     expl:"The first half is <code>hoare_assign</code>&rsquo;s argument done by hand, with the store fact discharged by computing <code>Store.set σ x 1</code> at <code>x</code>. The second half chooses the smallest counterexample there is: the store that is zero everywhere, and no memory at all. The precondition of the framed triple is then satisfied by cutting the empty heap into two empty heaps, and the postcondition, read at the final store, asserts both <code>1 = 1</code> and <code>1 = 0</code>. Only the second is used.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'constructor', h:"Split the conjunction into the triple and its negation. They share nothing but the variable."},
       {tac:'intro σ h ⟨_, he⟩', h:"Opened the triple: a store, a heap, and the precondition destructured. <code>pure φ</code> is <code>aAnd (fact φ) emp</code>, so the pattern takes two components; the store fact is discarded, because the value assigned is a constant and the postcondition follows from the assignment alone, and <code>he : emp σ h</code> is kept, because the postcondition is also a <code>pure</code> and needs it back."},
       {tac:'refine ⟨⟨Store.set σ x 1, h⟩, Exec.assign, ?_, he⟩', h:"Named the final state, gave the derivation, handed <code>emp</code> straight back, and left the store fact as the one hole. The heap is unchanged throughout — an assignment does not touch it."},
       {tac:'show Store.set σ x 1 x = 1', h:"Restated the hole. What Lean was displaying was <code>fact (fun σ => σ x = 1)</code> applied to two projections out of the state literal; the <code>show</code> replaces that with the arithmetic underneath it, which is definitionally the same goal and readable."},
       {tac:'simp [Store.set]', h:"Closed it: <code>Store.set</code> is a conditional and the condition is <code>x = x</code>."},
       {tac:'intro hall', h:"For the negation: assume the framed triple. The goal becomes <code>False</code>, and <code>hall</code> is now a function you may apply at any store, heap and proof of the precondition."},
       {tac:'obtain ⟨s\', hex, h₁, h₂, _, _, hq, hr⟩ := hall (fun _ => 0) Heap.empty ⟨…⟩', h:"Applied it and took the result apart in one line. The argument is the precondition at the empty heap: two empty heaps, disjoint by <code>disjoint_empty_left</code>, whose union is the empty heap by <code>union_empty_left</code> reversed, with the store fact true at both. Eight names come back, of which two matter: <code>hq</code> and <code>hr</code>, the two halves of the postcondition, both read at <code>s'.store</code>."},
       {tac:'cases hex', h:"Inversion on the run, which replaces <code>s'</code> by the state the assignment rule produces. Now <code>hr</code> says the store <code>Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1</code> holds <code>0</code> at <code>x</code>."},
       {tac:'have e0 : Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1 x = 0 := hr.1', h:"Restated <code>hr.1</code> as the equation it definitionally is. Without this the hypothesis displays folded through <code>fact</code> and through two state projections, and no rewrite finds anything in it."},
       {tac:'rw [show Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1 x = 1 from by simp [Store.set]] at e0', h:"Rewrote the left-hand side of <code>e0</code> using a fact proved on the spot: that same lookup is <code>1</code>. The hypothesis becomes <code>e0 : 1 = 0</code>. <code>show T from e</code> is the term-level form of the <code>show</code> tactic — it hands Lean the proof <code>e</code> at the type <code>T</code> you wrote — and <code>rw</code> wants a term, not a tactic block, which is why the equation is built here rather than named on a line of its own."},
       {tac:'exact absurd e0 (by simp)', h:"Two numerals, distinct. <code>False</code> follows."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'The refutation, three steps',
        start:"case right\nx : Var\nhall :\n  Hoare ((_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0) ∗ _root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0) (Cmd.assign x (Atom.const 1))\n    ((_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 1) ∗ _root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)\n⊢ False",
        steps:[
          {tac:'obtain ⟨s\', hex, h₁, h₂, _, _, hq, hr⟩ := hall … ',
           state:"s' : State\nhex : Exec (Cmd.assign x (Atom.const 1)) { store := fun x => 0, heap := Heap.empty } s'\nh₁ h₂ : Heap\nleft✝¹ : h₁.disjoint h₂\nleft✝ : s'.heap = h₁.union h₂\nhq : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 1) s'.store h₁\nhr : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) s'.store h₂\n⊢ False",
           h:"Only the new hypotheses are shown; <code>x</code> and <code>hall</code> are above them, unchanged. The two underscores in the pattern became <code>left✝</code> and <code>left✝¹</code>, which is fine — they are the disjointness and the cut, and neither is used. The lines to look at are the last two: <code>hq</code> and <code>hr</code> are both read at <code>s'.store</code>, the <i>same</i> store, and they say opposite things about it."},
          {tac:'cases hex',
           state:"case right.assign\n⊢ False",
           h:"The <code>⊢</code> line is unchanged; what changes is the hypotheses, and the whole state grows to thirty-one lines, because <code>s'</code> is replaced everywhere by a state literal built out of projections of another state literal. <code>hr</code> now says that the store <code>Store.set (fun x => 0) x 1</code> holds <code>0</code> at <code>x</code>."},
          {tac:'have e0 : Store.set (fun _ => 0) x 1 x = 0 := hr.1',
           state:"e0 : Store.set (fun x => 0) x 1 x = 0\n⊢ False",
           h:"The same fact with the wrapping taken off, ready to be rewritten. This is the only tactic in the proof whose job is legibility, and without it the two remaining lines have nothing to act on."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'},
       {t:'p', h:"Both halves of the postcondition are read at <code>s'.store</code> — the star cuts the <i>heap</i> and hands the same store to both conjuncts. That is the whole mechanism of the failure, and it goes back to the definition of <code>∗</code> in Unit 14 and, before that, to the decision in Unit 18 that the store is not a resource. A logic in which the store also divided would not have this problem and would not be able to say anything about a program variable either."}
     ],
     pitfall:"Reaching for <code>exact absurd hr.1 (by simp)</code> and skipping the two lines that restate the hypothesis. It looks like a saving and Lean answers <code>unsolved goals</code>, pointing at the <code>by simp</code> and leaving <code>⊢ ¬fact (fun σ => σ x = 0) (Store.set (fun x => 0) x (Atom.eval (fun x => 0) (Atom.const 1))) h₂</code>. That is the refutation <code>absurd</code> asked for, and <code>simp</code> cannot produce it: the proposition is folded inside <code>fact</code> and the store application has not been carried out, so there is nothing in it for a rewrite to match. Restating the fact at the type you want — <code>have e0 : … := hr.1</code>, with the same proof term — is what takes the wrapping off, and it costs nothing, because the two types are definitionally equal.",
     variants:"Two other correct statements. <b>With <code>fact</code> in place of <code>pure</code>:</b> the refutation still compiles, and every tuple in it loses a layer, because <code>fact φ</code> is not a pair: the precondition handed to the framed triple ends <code>…, rfl, rfl⟩</code> rather than <code>…, ⟨rfl, rfl⟩, ⟨rfl, rfl⟩⟩</code>, and the false store fact arrives as <code>hr</code> rather than <code>hr.1</code>. What it loses is the connection to Unit 23 — the rules whose composition this is about are stated with <code>pure</code>, so the counterexample about <code>pure</code> is the one that refutes those rules. <b>Split into two theorems:</b> the positive triple and the negation, stated separately. Equally correct, and it hides what the pair is for: a refutation of a rule needs a true premise, or it refutes nothing. Unit 07&rsquo;s <code>x16</code> and <code>x18</code> are the same pairing at assertion level. &middot; Now change one thing and the theorem is <b>false</b>: frame a fact about a <i>different</i> variable. <code>Hoare (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ y = 0)) (.assign x (.const 1)) (pure (fun σ => σ x = 1) ∗ pure (fun σ => σ y = 0))</code> is provable from <code>hne : y ≠ x</code>, in six tactic lines, and the only new step is <code>rw [storeSet_other σ x y 1 hne]</code>. That is the whole content of the side condition that comes next: what matters is not that <code>R</code> mentions the store, but that it mentions the part of the store this command writes."
    },

    /* ============================================== Preserves ================= */

    {t:'p', h:"So the frame rule needs a second hypothesis beyond the triple it starts from, and it is not the same kind of thing as locality. Locality is a claim about the command alone. This one cannot be: the counterexample turns on the command writing the very variable the assertion reads, so neither the command nor the assertion decides it on its own. It is a condition on the pair."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"Non-interference, semantically. For every run of <code>c</code>, and every heap at all, an assertion that held of the initial store still holds of the final one.",
     src:"def Preserves (c : Cmd) (R : Assertion) : Prop :=\n  ∀ s s', Exec c s s' → ∀ hFrame, R s.store hFrame → R s'.store hFrame"},

    {t:'p', h:"The heap argument is a bound variable, <code>hFrame</code>, and the same one on both sides of the arrow. That is the design decision in this definition and it is forced by the goal it has to close. In the frame proof the assertion is being read at <code>hR</code> — the frame — while the states <code>s</code> and <code>s'</code> carry the <i>whole</i> heap, <code>hP.union hR</code> and whatever the command left. Quantifying the heap separately is what lets one definition serve every cut."},

    {t:'detail', title:'The version that reads the assertion at the state&rsquo;s own heap', tag:'aside', open:false,
     blocks:[
       {t:'p', h:"The shorter definition is the one you would write first: an assertion that held of the initial state still holds of the final one."},
       {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"Shorter, and it does not fit.",
        src:"def PreservesBad (c : Cmd) (R : Assertion) : Prop :=\n  ∀ s s', Exec c s s' → R s.store s.heap → R s'.store s'.heap"},
       {t:'p', h:"Finish the frame proof with it in place of <code>Preserves</code> and the last line reports:"},
       {t:'state', cap:"The argument you have is <code>R</code> at the frame; the argument wanted is <code>R</code> at the whole heap. Those are different claims, and the second is false in general — <code>R</code> is an exact assertion about a piece of memory, and the whole heap is bigger than that piece.",
        src:"error: Application type mismatch: The argument\n  hr\nhas type\n  R σ hR\nbut is expected to have type\n  R { store := σ, heap := hP.union hR }.store { store := σ, heap := hP.union hR }.heap\nin the application\n  hpres { store := σ, heap := hP.union hR } r hrex hr"},
       {t:'p', h:"The repair is not to weaken <code>R</code> but to stop tying the heap to the state. Once the heap is its own binder, the definition says what it should have said all along: <i>whatever heap you read this assertion at, running the command does not change the answer</i>."}
     ]},

    /* ------------------------------------------- syntactic vs semantic ------- */

    {t:'p', h:"A textbook states this condition differently, and the difference is not a matter of taste."},

    {t:'cmp',
     left:{t:'What a textbook writes', kind:'bad',
       h:"<code>modifies(c) ∩ freeVars(R) = ∅</code>. Two functions from syntax to sets of variables: the variables the command assigns to, and the variables the assertion mentions. Both are computed by walking a tree. The condition is decidable, checkable by eye, and lets a tool discharge it silently."},
     right:{t:'What this course can write', kind:'good',
       h:"<code>modifies(c)</code> is fine — <code>Cmd</code> is an inductive type and you could write the recursion this afternoon. <code>freeVars(R)</code> is not, and the obstruction is not difficulty: an <code>Assertion</code> is <code>Store → Heap → Prop</code>, an arbitrary function. There is no tree to walk. The type has no syntax, so there is nothing to take the free variables <i>of</i>.",
       src:"abbrev Assertion := Store → Heap → Prop"}},

    {t:'p', h:"That is a consequence of the shallow embedding Unit 12 chose when it made an assertion a function rather than a piece of syntax, and the cost is real, because a syntactic side condition would be more convenient. It is also, on this particular point, a better condition than the syntactic one. Free variables are a property of a piece of text, not of the thing it denotes, and two texts denoting the same assertion can mention different variables:"},

    {t:'code', tag:'illustration', cap:"Two assertions that entail each other in both directions, one of which mentions variable <code>0</code> and one of which mentions no variable at all. A side condition computed from the text would give them different answers; a semantic one cannot tell them apart, because there is nothing there to tell apart.",
     src:"theorem same_assertion_two_spellings :\n    (fun _ _ => True : Assertion) ⊣⊢ (fun σ _ => σ 0 = σ 0) :=\n  ⟨fun _ _ _ => rfl, fun _ _ _ => True.intro⟩"},

    {t:'p', h:"The price of the semantic condition is that it is a proof obligation rather than a check: every application of the frame rule will owe a <code>Preserves c R</code>, and nothing discharges it automatically. The rest of this unit is about making that bill small."},

    /* ============================================== discharging Preserves ==== */

    {t:'sec', s:'Paying for it once'},

    {t:'p', h:"<code>Preserves</code> is indexed by a command and an assertion, so in the worst case there is one obligation per application of the frame rule. The worst case is not what happens, because most of the assertions you frame do not look at the store at all. <code>l ↦ v</code> is an equation on the heap; <code>emp</code> is an equation on the heap; a <code>∗</code> of those is a claim about how the heap comes apart. None of them can tell one store from another, and for an assertion like that the obligation is discharged without looking at the command."},

    {t:'code', tag:'verified', cap:"The condition, and the theorem that turns it into <code>Preserves</code> for every command at once.",
     src:"def HeapOnly (R : Assertion) : Prop := ∀ σ σ' h, R σ h → R σ' h\n\ntheorem preserves_of_heapOnly {R : Assertion} (c : Cmd) (h : HeapOnly R) : Preserves c R :=\n  fun s s' _ hFrame hr => h s.store s'.store hFrame hr"},

    {t:'p', h:"The underscore in the proof term is the derivation. <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> takes a run of <code>c</code> and throws it away, and that is the whole of its strength: it holds for every command, including commands nobody has written yet. Any language extension that leaves <code>Assertion</code> alone inherits it unchanged."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x49',
     name:'heapOnly_pointsTo / heapOnly_emp / heapOnly_star',
     hard:false,
     why:"These three are what make the frame rule cheap. Every specification in the rest of this course frames an assertion built out of <code>↦</code>, <code>emp</code> and <code>∗</code>, and each of those framings needs a <code>Preserves</code> that these three plus <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> supply for nothing. The counts, grepped: <code>heapOnly_pointsTo</code> is cited four times after this unit — twice in Unit 27, once in Unit 28, once in Unit 29 — and <code>heapOnly_emp</code> and <code>heapOnly_star</code> are never cited again, because every frame in the worked programs of this course is a single cell. <code>heapOnly_star</code> is what makes that a fact about the examples rather than about the theory: it takes any two heap-only assertions to a heap-only assertion, with no bound on how many cells are involved, so a frame of any size costs the same nothing.",
     setup:"Three statements. The first two are one term each and contain no tactic; if you find yourself writing <code>by</code>, look again at what the assertion actually says about its store argument. The third is a destructuring you have done many times, with the six slots of <code>∗</code> going out in the same order they came in.",
     goal:"theorem heapOnly_pointsTo (l : Loc) (v : Val) : HeapOnly (l ↦ v) := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem heapOnly_emp : HeapOnly emp := by\n  sorry\n\ntheorem heapOnly_star {P Q : Assertion} (hp : HeapOnly P) (hq : HeapOnly Q) :\n    HeapOnly (P ∗ Q) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, <code>HeapOnly R</code> says: for all stores <code>σ</code> and <code>σ'</code> and every heap <code>h</code>, if <code>R σ h</code> then <code>R σ' h</code>. For the first goal <code>R</code> is <code>l ↦ v</code>, whose body is <code>fun _ h => h = Heap.singleton l v</code>: an equation in which the store binder is written <code>_</code>.",
       "If the store plays no part in the statement, then the statement at <code>σ'</code> is the statement at <code>σ</code> — the same proposition, not an equivalent one. So the proof is the identity function. For the star: a proof that <code>(P ∗ Q) σ h</code> is a cut of <code>h</code> together with proofs of <code>P</code> and <code>Q</code> at the halves. The cut is a fact about <code>h</code> and survives; the two halves are repaired by the two hypotheses.",
       "No tactic is wanted for the first two. A proof of <code>HeapOnly R</code> is a function of four arguments — two stores, a heap, and a proof of <code>R</code> at the first store — returning a proof at the second; for these two assertions the fourth argument already has the type of the result, so write a term and let Lean check it by reduction. The third does need <code>intro</code>, with the six-name pattern for the star in the same line, and then one anonymous-constructor term.",
       "<code>fun _ _ _ hr => hr</code> is the whole of each of the first two. For the star, <code>intro σ σ' h ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, h1, h2⟩</code> leaves <code>⊢ (P ∗ Q) σ' h</code> with all six components of the incoming cut named. The outgoing tuple is <code>⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, ?_, ?_⟩</code>, and the two holes are <code>hp σ σ' h₁ h1</code> and <code>hq σ σ' h₂ h2</code>."
     ],
     sol:"theorem heapOnly_pointsTo (l : Loc) (v : Val) : HeapOnly (l ↦ v) :=\n  fun _ _ _ hr => hr\n\ntheorem heapOnly_emp : HeapOnly emp := fun _ _ _ hr => hr\n\ntheorem heapOnly_star {P Q : Assertion} (hp : HeapOnly P) (hq : HeapOnly Q) :\n    HeapOnly (P ∗ Q) := by\n  intro σ σ' h ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, h1, h2⟩\n  exact ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp σ σ' h₁ h1, hq σ σ' h₂ h2⟩",
     solNote:"That the first two are the identity is the dividend of a decision made eleven units ago. Unit 13 wrote <code>l ↦ v</code> as an equation on the heap with the store binder discarded, rather than as a lookup with a store in it. Had it gone the other way, these two proofs would still be short — but they would have content, and every one of them in the rest of the course would have to be read.",
     expl:"<code>HeapOnly R</code> asks for a function taking a proof of <code>R</code> at one store to a proof at another. When <code>R</code>&rsquo;s body ignores its store argument the two propositions are literally the same, so the identity has the right type and Lean accepts it without unfolding anything. <code>heapOnly_star</code> is the induction step of that observation over the one connective that builds big assertions from small ones: the cut of the heap has no store in it either, so it travels; the two conjuncts are repaired by the hypotheses.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'fun _ _ _ hr => hr', h:"Three binders — the two stores and the heap — none of them named, because none of them is used. <code>hr</code> is a proof of <code>h = Heap.singleton l v</code>, and so is the thing the goal wants."},
       {tac:'fun _ _ _ hr => hr', h:"The same term a second time, at <code>emp</code>, where the premise and the conclusion are both <code>h = Heap.empty</code>. Two different assertions with literally the same proof is the signal worth reading: neither of them is doing anything with the store it is handed, so neither can tell one store from another."},
       {tac:'intro σ σ\' h ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, h1, h2⟩', h:"Discharged the three binders of <code>HeapOnly</code> and destructured the premise in the same line. The premise is <code>(P ∗ Q) σ h</code>, so the pattern has six names: two heaps, their disjointness, the equation cutting <code>h</code>, and the two assertions. The goal is the same star at <code>σ'</code>."},
       {tac:'exact ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp σ σ\' h₁ h1, hq σ σ\' h₂ h2⟩', h:"Rebuilt the star with the same cut. The first four components are copied unchanged — they say nothing about a store. The last two are the incoming proofs pushed through the hypotheses, which is where the recursion in this argument lives."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'heapOnly_star, both states',
        start:"P Q : Assertion\nhp : HeapOnly P\nhq : HeapOnly Q\n⊢ HeapOnly (P ∗ Q)",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro σ σ\' h hstar',
           state:"P Q : Assertion\nhp : HeapOnly P\nhq : HeapOnly Q\nσ σ' : Store\nh : Heap\nhstar : (P ∗ Q) σ h\n⊢ (P ∗ Q) σ' h",
           h:"The whole exercise in two lines: the same heap, two different stores. Shown here with the premise left whole, so that the shape is visible before it is taken apart."},
          {tac:'obtain ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, h1, h2⟩ := hstar',
           state:"P Q : Assertion\nhp : HeapOnly P\nhq : HeapOnly Q\nσ σ' : Store\nh h₁ h₂ : Heap\nhd : h₁.disjoint h₂\nhu : h = h₁.union h₂\nh1 : P σ h₁\nh2 : Q σ h₂\n⊢ (P ∗ Q) σ' h",
           h:"The solution folds these two lines into one by putting the pattern inside the <code>intro</code>; they are separated here so that both states print. Now look at which hypotheses mention a store. <code>hd</code> and <code>hu</code> do not, so they go straight into the answer. <code>h1</code> and <code>h2</code> do, and each has a hypothesis waiting for it."},
          {tac:'exact ⟨h₁, h₂, hd, hu, hp σ σ\' h₁ h1, hq σ σ\' h₂ h2⟩', state:'No goals.', h:"Six slots, four of them the same terms that came in."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'}
     ],
     pitfall:"Getting the two stores the wrong way round in the last two slots of <code>heapOnly_star</code>. <code>HeapOnly P</code> reads <code>∀ σ σ' h, P σ h → P σ' h</code>, so the store you <i>have</i> is the first argument and the store you <i>want</i> is the second; write <code>hp σ' σ h₁ h1</code> and Lean answers <code>Application type mismatch: The argument h1 has type P σ h₁ but is expected to have type P σ' h₁</code>. Nothing in the shape of the tuple prevents this — both arguments are stores, both are in scope, and the swap is caught only when the premise is checked. &middot; The other one is harmless: writing the first two as <code>by intro σ σ' h hr; exact hr</code> instead of a term works fine. It stops working the moment the assertion <i>is</i> about the store, where the same line reports <code>Type mismatch: hr has type _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) σ h but is expected to have type _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) σ' h</code> — and that is the next exercise in one line.",
     variants:"Drop <code>hq</code> from <code>heapOnly_star</code> and keep <code>hp</code>: the last slot fails with <code>The argument h2 has type Q σ h₂ but is expected to have type Q σ' h₂</code>, and there is nothing else in the context to close it with. Both halves of a star have to be heap-only for the star to be, and the result is false without it: <code>¬ HeapOnly (emp ∗ pure (fun σ => σ x = 0))</code> is provable in six lines, by the argument the next exercise sets, applied to the second half of the cut. &middot; <code>aTrue</code> is heap-only, by the same identity term, and so is <code>pure (fun _ => (0 : Nat) = 0)</code>: what matters is not the shape of the assertion but whether its truth can vary with the store. &middot; <code>fact φ</code> is the extreme case in the other direction — it ignores the <i>heap</i>, and reading <code>HeapOnly</code> at it gives <code>∀ σ σ' h, φ σ → φ σ'</code>, in which the heap binder is idle, so it is heap-only exactly when <code>φ</code> has the same truth value at every store."
    },

    {t:'p', h:"So the route is free for every assertion the small-footprint rules produce: cells, the empty heap, and any number of them starred together, at any size. It stops in one place, and it stops exactly where the frame rule failed."},

    {t:'ex',
     id:'x50',
     name:'not_heapOnly_pure',
     hard:true,
     why:"Knowing which framings are free is worth as much as knowing that some are, and this fixes the boundary. Four of the <code>Preserves</code> obligations in the rest of the course are discharged by <code>preserves_of_heapOnly</code> applied to a single cell. The two that are not are both about a fact concerning a program variable — one a general lemma in Unit 29, one a specific program in Unit 30 — and both need a different sufficient condition, about which variables the command writes rather than about the assertion alone. This exercise is what makes that second condition necessary rather than convenient.",
     setup:"A refutation, so you choose the witnesses: two stores and a heap. Everything is as small as it can be — the assertion owns no memory, so the heap is <code>Heap.empty</code>, and the two stores are constants. Four lines: assume, instantiate, restate, contradict. The third of them proves nothing and is there only to make a hypothesis readable.",
     goal:"theorem not_heapOnly_pure (x : Var) : ¬ HeapOnly (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)) := by",
     hints:[
       "Unfolded, the goal is: assuming that for all <code>σ</code>, <code>σ'</code> and <code>h</code>, <code>pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) σ h</code> implies <code>pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) σ' h</code>, derive <code>False</code>. <code>pure φ</code> is <code>aAnd (fact φ) emp</code>, so each of those is a pair: the store fact, and the heap being empty.",
       "The assumption says a store fact survives a change of store. So choose a store in which it is true and one in which it is false, apply the assumption, and read off the contradiction. The premise you have to supply is that the first store holds <code>0</code> at <code>x</code> and the heap is empty — both by computation, if you pick constant stores.",
       "<code>intro</code> turns the negation into an assumption and the goal into <code>False</code>. Three moves after that. Apply the assumption to two stores, a heap and a proof of its premise — the premise is a pair, and if the stores you pick are constant both halves are <code>rfl</code>. Restate the store fact that comes back, using <code>have</code> with the type written out, because it arrives displayed folded through <code>fact</code> and nothing can act on it in that form. Then <code>absurd</code>.",
       "<code>intro hho</code> leaves <code>⊢ False</code> with <code>hho : HeapOnly (_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)</code>. Then <code>have h := hho (fun _ => 0) (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩</code> gives you the assertion at the second store, and <code>h.1</code> is the false half — but it is displayed folded through <code>fact</code>, so the next line restates it."
     ],
     sol:"theorem not_heapOnly_pure (x : Var) : ¬ HeapOnly (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)) := by\n  intro hho\n  have h := hho (fun _ => 0) (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩\n  have h1 : (1 : Nat) = 0 := h.1\n  exact absurd h1 (by simp)",
     solNote:"The store <code>fun _ => 1</code> makes the fact false at every variable at once, which is why <code>x</code> never has to be chosen or compared with anything. A refutation is allowed to be that blunt.",
     expl:"<code>pure φ</code> is exact about memory and says nothing about which stores it holds at — that is the point of it, and it is why Unit 23 could put a store fact in a postcondition without owning any heap. The same freedom makes it store-dependent, and <code>HeapOnly</code> is exactly the denial of store-dependence. The two constant stores turn the assumption into <code>1 = 0</code>.",
     walk:[
       {tac:'intro hho', h:"Turned the negation into an assumption. <code>¬ P</code> is <code>P → False</code>, so the goal is now <code>False</code> and <code>hho</code> is a function of three arguments and a premise."},
       {tac:'have h := hho (fun _ => 0) (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩', h:"Instantiated it. The two stores are constants; the heap is empty; the premise <code>pure φ (fun _ => 0) Heap.empty</code> is a pair whose halves are <code>(fun _ => 0) x = 0</code> and <code>Heap.empty = Heap.empty</code>, both true by reduction, hence <code>⟨rfl, rfl⟩</code>. What comes back is the same assertion at the store that is <code>1</code> everywhere."},
       {tac:'have h1 : (1 : Nat) = 0 := h.1', h:"Took the store half and restated it at the type it definitionally has. <code>h.1</code> displays as <code>fact (fun σ => σ x = 0) (fun x => 1) Heap.empty</code>; the <code>have</code> says <i>that is the proposition <code>1 = 0</code></i>, and Lean checks it by reduction. Nothing is proved on this line; a name is put on a fact in a readable form."},
       {tac:'exact absurd h1 (by simp)', h:"<code>absurd</code> takes a proof and a refutation. <code>simp</code> knows two distinct numerals are distinct, so it supplies <code>¬ (1 = 0)</code>, and the two together are <code>False</code>."}
     ],
     deep:[
       {t:'trace', title:'Three states, and why the third line exists',
        start:"x : Var\n⊢ ¬HeapOnly (_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)",
        steps:[
          {tac:'intro hho',
           state:"x : Var\nhho : HeapOnly (_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)\n⊢ False",
           h:"<code>_root_.pure</code> is how our <code>pure</code> prints once Lean&rsquo;s own <code>pure</code> is in scope, as Unit 17 said it would be."},
          {tac:'have h := hho (fun _ => 0) (fun _ => 1) Heap.empty ⟨rfl, rfl⟩',
           state:"x : Var\nhho : HeapOnly (_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)\nh : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) (fun x => 1) Heap.empty\n⊢ False",
           h:"The contradiction is already in the context: <code>h</code> says a store that is <code>1</code> everywhere holds <code>0</code> at <code>x</code>. The binder in <code>fun x => 1</code> is Lean&rsquo;s own naming and has nothing to do with the variable <code>x</code> beside it."},
          {tac:'have h1 : (1 : Nat) = 0 := h.1',
           state:"x : Var\nhho : HeapOnly (_root_.pure fun σ => σ x = 0)\nh : _root_.pure (fun σ => σ x = 0) (fun x => 1) Heap.empty\nh1 : 1 = 0\n⊢ False",
           h:"Two folds came off at once — <code>pure</code>&rsquo;s first projection and <code>fact</code> — and the store application reduced. Compare <code>h1</code> with the shape of <code>h.1</code> without the restatement: <code>fact (fun σ => σ x = 0) (fun x => 1) Heap.empty</code>, which is the same proposition and has nothing for a tactic to grip."}
        ],
        done:'No goals.'}
     ],
     pitfall:"<code>exact absurd h.1 (by simp)</code>, skipping the restatement. Lean answers <code>`simp` made no progress</code> — not a complaint about the mathematics but about the display: <code>simp</code> is looking at <code>fact (fun σ => σ x = 0) (fun x => 1) Heap.empty</code>, whose head is a definition it has not been told to unfold, and there is no equation in sight. The fix is the <code>have</code> with the type written out, which is the same trick Unit 22 needed on <code>assign_twice</code>.",
     variants:"Make <code>φ</code> constant and the theorem is false: <code>HeapOnly (pure (fun _ => (0 : Nat) = 0))</code> is proved by <code>fun _ _ _ hr => hr</code>, the same identity as <code>heapOnly_emp</code>. What fails is not <code>pure</code> but store-dependence, and the definition of <code>HeapOnly</code> is blind to which of the two you have. &middot; The same refutation works for <code>fact (fun σ => σ x = 0)</code>, one line shorter, since <code>fact φ</code> is not a pair. &middot; Try to prove <code>HeapOnly</code> of a store-dependent assertion by <code>intro σ σ' h hr; exact hr</code> and the error names the store argument directly — that is the same failure, reported before you have chosen any witnesses at all. &middot; What this refutes is <code>HeapOnly</code>, not <code>Preserves</code>, and they fail in different places. <code>Preserves .skip (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0))</code> is three lines — <code>intro s s' hex hFrame hr</code>, <code>cases hex</code>, <code>exact hr</code> — because the only state <code>skip</code> reaches is the one it started in. <code>¬ Preserves (.assign x (.const 1)) (pure (fun σ => σ x = 0))</code> is provable by this exercise&rsquo;s own argument, run at the single store that command produces. A store-dependent assertion is never heap-only; it is still preserved by every command that cannot falsify it, and that gap is the size of the condition Unit 29 has to supply."
    },

    {t:'p', h:"The assertion that is not heap-only is the assertion that broke the frame rule, and they are the same fact seen from two sides. <code>pure (fun σ => σ x = 0)</code> can change truth value when the store changes; a command that changes the store can therefore falsify it; so it needs a <code>Preserves</code> that nothing on this page supplies. What Unit 29 adds is the other sufficient condition — one that looks at the command instead of the assertion, and asks whether the variables it writes are variables the assertion reads. The last <i>variants</i> panel above is that condition in miniature: framing <code>y = 0</code> onto <code>x := 1</code> is sound, and the proof is a single application of <code>storeSet_other</code>."},

    /* ===================================================== close ============= */

    {t:'sec', s:'Where that leaves the frame rule'},

    {t:'dod',
     h:"You can state the frame rule and say why it is a claim about <code>Exec</code>. You have written the textbook locality condition as a <code>def</code>, read it as a commuting square, seen the version without the existential and what it costs, and found by proof rather than by inspection that the textbook version is one conjunct short of what the frame rule consumes. You have proved the strengthened version of three commands. You have refuted the frame rule outright for an assertion about a program variable, and turned that refutation into the second hypothesis, <code>Preserves</code> — stated semantically, because the syntactic condition cannot be written at this type at all. And you can discharge it for nothing for any assertion built from <code>↦</code>, <code>emp</code> and <code>∗</code>, at any size, and say precisely where that stops."},

    {t:'p', h:"Two definitions, both justified, and the frame rule they were designed for still unproved. Locality is a claim about <code>Exec</code>, and <code>Exec</code> has ten constructors. The three whose commands hand the heap back as they found it are done. The two that change it are where the argument has content, and each of them is a <code>funext</code> over a union."}

  ]
});
